th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/revenue-equivalence-theorem.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/revenue-equivalence-theorem.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_revenue_equivalence_theorem_e439e270_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The revenue equivalence theorem states that expected revenue equals $\\mathbb{E}[\\theta_{(n-1)}]$, where $\\theta_{(1)}\\le\\theta_{(2)}\\le\\cdots\\le\\theta_{(n)}$ are the $n$ bidders' values sorted from smallest to largest. Which value is $\\theta_{(n-1)}$?","options":["The second-highest of the $n$ values","The second-lowest of the $n$ values","The value of the bidder labelled $n-1$","The highest value among bidder 1's $n-1$ rivals"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1fcd137da14d574d56b26566","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_revenue_equivalence_theorem_e439e270_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In an auction format satisfying the hypotheses of the revenue equivalence theorem, the envelope argument gives a type's equilibrium expected surplus as $U(\\theta)=U(\\underline\\theta)+\\int_{\\underline\\theta}^{\\theta}G(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Which two ingredients therefore determine $U(\\theta)$ for every type?","options":["The win probability $G$ fixed by the allocation and the lowest type's surplus","The equilibrium bidding function and the format's payment rule","The win probability $G$ and the price the winner pays in each realization","The lowest type's surplus and the bid of the highest type $\\overline\\theta$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0d5bc5a32be54e5c0cf85da2","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_revenue_equivalence_theorem_e439e270_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Four risk-neutral bidders have i.i.d. $U[0,1]$ private values, and an auction format satisfies the hypotheses of the revenue equivalence theorem, so its expected revenue is $\\mathbb{E}[\\theta_{(n-1)}]$. For $U[0,1]$ values, $\\mathbb{E}[\\theta_{(k)}]=\\frac{k}{n+1}$. What is the expected revenue?","options":["$\\frac{4}{5}$","$\\frac{3}{5}$","$\\frac{2}{5}$","$\\frac{3}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cb17f4f4e6d9d8ba396db2f0","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}